2. What does “real-time data for every login” actually mean?

This is an important part of your project.

All three dashboards can display live updates from the same database. However, real-time updates do not automatically mean live electricity measurements.

Your platform should distinguish between these sources:

Data type	Source	Update behavior
Energy consumption	Verified imported records or a genuine authorized energy API	Update after successful imports or when the real source provides new readings
Analytics	Calculations using database records	Recalculate when relevant data changes
Data quality	Actual validation and import results	Update after validation or import
Geographic map	Stored coordinates and associated energy records	Refresh when the underlying records change
Planning actions	PostgreSQL	Update after a successful create or edit
User management	PostgreSQL	Update after authorized account changes
System health	Backend and database health checks	Refresh periodically
Weather, if included	A configured external weather API	Refresh according to the provider's update frequency

For example, when a Data Manager uploads and successfully imports a dataset, the Energy Analyst should see the updated consumption totals, area comparisons, and map data without manually entering those figures.

A planner should also see updated priorities when the underlying records change.

You can achieve this with database-backed APIs and periodic refresh, or Server-Sent Events/WebSockets if genuinely needed. For your deadline, database-backed APIs with sensible refresh intervals are the simpler starting point.

Do not claim live electricity data unless a genuine source supplies it. If your submission dataset is static, label it as historical or demonstration data.

